\section{Requires the optional \{chromote\} headless-browser transport}{

\strong{This is the only handler in the family that cannot run on a pure-R
install.} The SABIA portal \emph{TLS-fingerprints} the client: \code{libcurl}'s
ClientHello is rejected before any HTTP is exchanged, so \code{httr2} cannot even
complete the handshake. The handler therefore reaches the portal exclusively
through the optional headless-browser transport (a real headless Chrome
driven by the \strong{\{chromote\}} package, listed in \code{Suggests}). The browser
navigates to the portal origin — clearing the TLS gate the way a real browser
does and establishing the Liferay session cookie — and then runs the portal's
own \code{fetch()} / form submissions in-page so every request rides Chrome's TLS
stack and cookies. All parsing stays in R.

If \{chromote\} or a Chrome/Chromium binary is not available the handler
aborts up front with an actionable message (class
\code{planscanR_error_browser_unavailable}). Install \{chromote\} and Google Chrome
(or set \code{options(planscanR.chrome_path=)} / the \code{CHROMOTE_CHROME} environment
variable) to enable it. \strong{Every other country works without a browser.}
}

\section{URL enumeration}{

One session per register. The handler opens the register's origin (which
clears the TLS gate and sets the session cookie), harvests the per-session
\code{datosPropios} token from the search form, and issues a single bulk
\code{accion=proy_resultados} POST. The response is a multi-megabyte page whose
\verb{<table id="tablaResultados">} carries \strong{every} record as a row of
\verb{expediente code | title | estado de tramitación}. The handler parses all
rows in R, respecting the global \code{limit}, and reuses the same session for the
per-record document-panel (\emph{ficha}) navigation.
}

\section{Attachments}{

The portal's document URLs are \strong{session-bound and ephemeral} — a
\verb{BINARYPORTLET resource.process} URL is valid only inside the live session
that rendered the \emph{Acceso a la Documentación} panel (it carries per-render
\code{javax.portlet.sync} tokens), and the PDFs are themselves on the
TLS-fingerprinted host. So the handler does \strong{not} persist those live URLs.
Instead it stores a stable synthetic identity URL per document
(\verb{<origin>#<code>/<NOMBRE_SABIA>}) in \code{attachment_urls}, grouped by the
portal's \emph{Tipo de documento} into \verb{attachment_urls_<slug>} columns (the slug
is the ASCII-folded document-type label). When \code{download = TRUE}, the handler
navigates the live session to the record's \emph{listadoDocumentacion} page,
resolves each synthetic URL to the live resource URL in the DOM, and pulls the
bytes in-session via the browser (\code{browser_download()}), honouring
\code{max_file_size_mb}. A record whose ficha exposes no documents yields an empty
\code{attachment_urls} vector, which is valid.
}

\section{Geometry}{

None. SABIA exposes no public per-record geometry (no WMS/WFS), so
\code{geometry_path} / \code{geometry_crs} are never set.
